  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nIn previous forecasts, a warm front was expected to push north of \nthe area late Saturday, however looking at recent surface analysis \nthis boundary appears to have stayed south of the area. As a result, \nthe bulk of heavy precipitation has remained out of the area with \nthe highest totals reaching Toledo at almost 1\" so far. This warm \nfront may nudge a bit more north today as a low pressure system \nmoves along it towards the CWA. This cold front will move a cold \nfront across the area this afternoon. As a result, there remains an \nelevated potential for heavy rainfall, especially within any \nthunderstorms that develop, however the widespread heavy rain is no \nlonger expected as the primary precipitation mode should remain more \nstratiform tonight. As a result, have opted to lower QPF with this \nforecast package to generally be 0.3-0.8 inches of precipitation \nwith a higher axis around 1 inch extending from Toledo southeast \ntowards Mount Vernon. Some of these areas have already received a \ndecent bit of rain on Saturday which will result in saturated \nantecedent conditions. However, given recent low levels on area \nrivers and the more stratiform type of precipitation the area should \nmostly handle the additional rainfall. Overall, today will be quite \na dreary day and will also mark the transition into a week of fall \nlike conditions. Highs today will climb into the upper 60s across \nnorthern counties and into the mid to upper 70s across southern \ncounties. Overnight lows should drop into the low to mid 50s with \nthe exception of portions of NWPA which may even see temperatures \nfall into the 40s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nAs the system today departs east, a Canadian high pressure will \nnudge south across much of the Great Lakes region while a surface \ntrough linger across the Ohio Valley. The placement of these systems \nwill leave the CWA right in the middle. This positioning will allow \nfor a cooler airmass to arrive and high temperatures fall into the \n60s this week with overnight lows in the 40s to 50s. Chances for \nprecipitation on Monday should be isolated to the far southern \ncounties, but shower potential ramps back up on Tuesday and \nWednesday as an upper level shortwave trough moves east, nudging the \nsurface high north and providing support for the potential \ndevelopment of additional showers. This high will push back south \nfor the end of the work week. In addition, the positioning of the \narea on the gradient between the trough and high pressure will \nresult in breezy conditions this week with the strongest winds \nexpected along the I-75 corridor.",
